@node Dataflow
@section Dataflow

A @dfn{timely dataflow} is a directed graph of @dfn{operators} connected
by @dfn{streams}.  Each operator receives batches of records on its
input streams and produces batches on its output streams.  The graph may
contain cycles, so a computation can feed its output back into itself,
which is how iteration works.

A @dfn{worker} runs the whole dataflow graph.  A computation may run one
worker on the calling thread, several workers in one process, or many
workers across several processes; every worker runs the same dataflow
and processes a share of the data.  Workers exchange records across the
graph's partition boundaries through the communication layer selected by
the @code{dtfl_config} (@pxref{Installation}).

A program drives a worker explicitly: it builds dataflows with
@code{dtfl_worker_dataflow}, feeds inputs, and calls
@code{dtfl_worker_step} repeatedly.  Each step schedules the operators
that have work and advances progress.  The convenience entry point
@code{dtfl_execute} starts the configured workers, each on its own
thread, and runs a worker function on each.

@node Records
@section Records

Rust gets typed records from monomorphisation, which C cannot copy.
libdtfl instead treats a record as a byte string, described by a
@code{dtfl_type} descriptor (@pxref{API Reference}).  The descriptor
says how long a value is, how two values compare, how a value hashes for
exchange routing, and how to print one for debugging.

The library never inspects record bytes directly.  It calls the
descriptor's @code{cmp} (for arrangements, consolidation and the order
in which @code{reduce} sees values) and @code{hash} (for exchange) and
nothing else.  A descriptor's @code{cmp} must reproduce the Rust derived
@code{Ord} of the type it models, because user reducers depend on the
order their input arrives in.

The built-in descriptors use order-preserving encodings, so their
@code{cmp} is a byte comparison: the unsigned integers are big-endian;
the signed integers are big-endian with the sign bit flipped; bytes and
strings compare their raw payloads.  The accessors
@code{dtfl_put_u64}, @code{dtfl_get_u64} and the rest hide the encoding,
so callers always work with native values.  Composite descriptors
(@code{dtfl_type_tuple}, @code{dtfl_type_pair}, @code{dtfl_type_option},
@code{dtfl_type_vec}) build structured records from the built-ins.

A key/value collection stores a record as @code{key || val}, the key
concatenated with the value; the key descriptor's length gives the split
point.

@node Time and frontiers
@section Time and frontiers

Every record on a stream carries a @dfn{logical time}: the time at which
that record takes effect.  A time is a short vector of @code{uint64_t}
coordinates.  How many coordinates there are, and how two times compare,
come from the stream's scope, which carries a @code{dtfl_time_class}
(@pxref{API Reference}).

Time is not always totally ordered.  The built-in classes are:

@itemize @bullet
@item
the unit time (no coordinates; every time is equal), for a computation
with a single logical moment;
@item
a totally ordered integer or @code{Duration} time (one coordinate), the
usual choice for a stream of epochs;
@item
a product time @code{Product<outer, inner>}, which pairs an outer time
with an inner one and orders them componentwise; iterative scopes use a
product with a @code{u64} iteration counter;
@item
@code{AltNeu} and @code{PointStamp}, used by the worst-case-optimal
joins and the dynamic scopes.
@end itemize

A product order is a @emph{partial} order: two times can be
incomparable.  @code{dtfl_time_less_equal} tests the class's partial
order; @code{dtfl_time_cmp} is the total word order that matches Rust's
derived @code{Ord}.

A @dfn{frontier} is an antichain of times: a set of pairwise
incomparable times that marks the boundary between the past (times at or
before some frontier element) and the possible future.  An operator sees
its input's frontier; when the frontier passes a time, no more records
can arrive at or before that time, so any computation waiting on that
time may complete.

@node Progress
@section Progress

@dfn{Progress tracking} is how the runtime discovers that a time is
complete, even in a graph with cycles.  Each operator reports the times
it still holds the right to send at (its outstanding @dfn{capabilities}),
and the runtime combines those reports, following each stream's
@dfn{path summary} (the minimum amount a time advances as it traverses
the operator), to compute every input's frontier.

A program observes progress with a @dfn{probe}.
@code{dtfl_stream_probe_with} attaches a probe to a stream;
@code{dtfl_probe_less_than} asks whether the stream's frontier is still
behind a given time, and @code{dtfl_probe_done} asks whether the stream
has drained completely.  A typical driver loop advances its input, then
steps the worker until the probe reports that the input's time is
complete:

@example
dtfl_input_session_advance_to(input, &next_epoch);
dtfl_input_session_flush(input);
while (dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_session_time(input)))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
@end example

@node Collections
@section Collections

A @dfn{differential collection} is a multiset whose contents change over
time.  Internally it is a timely stream whose records are @dfn{updates}:
a @code{(record, time, diff)} triple.  The @var{record} is a byte string;
the @var{time} is when the change takes effect; the @var{diff} says how
the multiplicity of the record changes at that time.  Inserting a record
is a diff of @code{+1}; removing it is @code{-1}.  The collection at any
time is the sum of all diffs for each record at times at or before it.

The diff is a value of the collection's @dfn{diff class}.  The default
class is signed 64-bit (@code{dtfl_diff_i64}), which is @emph{Abelian}:
it can negate, so it supports retraction.  Other classes trade capability
for a weaker algebra: the unsigned classes are monoids (they add but do
not negate); the @code{present} class only records presence.  An
operator checks, when the dataflow is built, that the diff class has the
algebra it needs, and names the operator if it does not.

Because the contents are expressed as diffs, a change to an input flows
through the dataflow as diffs: only the records whose multiplicity
actually changed are recomputed.  This is what makes differential
dataflow @emph{incremental}.  The collection operators (@code{map},
@code{filter}, @code{join}, @code{reduce}, @code{iterate} and the rest)
are covered in @ref{Differential Dataflow}.
